<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<en-export export-date="20260617T182517Z" application="Evernote" version="10.120.3">
  <note>
    <title>JSON Web Tokens(JWT)</title>
    <note-attributes>
    </note-attributes>
    <content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><div>A JSON Web Token (JWT) is a compact, URL-safe object for securely transmitting information.  It reliably asserts claims between parties, ensuring data integrity and authenticity through digital signatures.  The token consists of three distinct parts: a header, a payload, and a signature.  The header specifies the token's type and the particular cryptographic algorithm employed for signing.  The payload securely contains the claims, which are statements about an entity or additional data.  Finally, the signature verifies the token's origin and ensures it has not been tampered with.  JWTs are frequently utilized for authentication and authorization in modern web applications.  They can also be encrypted to safeguard sensitive data within their payload, offering confidentiality.  The <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7519" rev="en_rl_none"><span style="color:rgb(24, 168, 65);">RFC 7519</span></a> defines the definitive specification for JSON Web Tokens.  Their self-contained nature makes them ideal for stateless communication, often in HTTP Authorization headers.</div><div><br/></div><div><b><a href="https://www.evernote.com/shard/s341/nl/54341568/f6248bc5-082c-4cd4-9a44-d1c19c8c68ce" rev="en_rl_none">Flow</a></b>:</div><div><b><a href="https://www.evernote.com/shard/s341/nl/54341568/ea547236-70c2-7007-d353-5d8a42ff6591?title=JWT%20Security" rel="noopener noreferrer" rev="en_rl_none">Security</a>:</b> information within the JSON object is verifiable and trusted due to its robust digital signature.  This signature ensures the integrity and authenticity of all contained claims.</div><div><b><a href="https://www.evernote.com/shard/s341/nl/54341568/c9323fe2-27e2-457f-8d33-ee0842ad3bc3" rel="noopener noreferrer" rev="en_rl_none">Components</a>:</b> these include a header, a payload, and a cryptographic signature.  Each part serves a distinct function within the token structure.</div><div><b>States</b>:</div><ul><li><div><b>Signed only</b> - providing integrity and authenticity, guaranteeing the token has not been tampered with.  This ensures it originates from a trusted source, crucial for verification.  The payload content, however, remains readable to any party receiving the token.</div></li><li><div><b>Signed and Encrypted</b> - providing both integrity and authenticity via the digital signature.  Confidentiality is also ensured through encryption, making the payload entirely unreadable without the correct decryption key.  This offers robust and comprehensive protection for sensitive information.</div></li></ul><div><b><a href="https://www.evernote.com/shard/s341/nl/54341568/c18c6b48-ff9f-d8a7-71b6-0fc80d96d84a?title=JWT%20Signing%20Algorithm" rel="noopener noreferrer" rev="en_rl_none">Signing Algorithm</a>:</b> done with either symmetric or asymmetric cryptographic algorithms.  These distinct types dictate key management and distribution strategies, influencing security.</div><ul><li><div><b>Symmetric -</b> simpler to implement but necessitates sharing the same secret key with all parties.  This shared secret makes them inherently less secure for broader distribution.  Compromise of the single key affects the integrity of all signed tokens.</div></li><li><div><b>Asymmetric </b>- algorithms use a cryptographic pair of public and private keys.  The authorization server typically uses the private key for signing, which is kept secret.  Resource servers then use the corresponding public key for verifying received signatures.  This secure separation enhances overall trust and system security significantly.</div></li></ul><div><b>Usage:</b> </div><ul><li><div><b><a href="https://www.evernote.com/shard/s341/nl/54341568/710eb64b-8510-46b4-ab4c-6426b0ec4012?title=Authorization" rev="en_rl_none">Authorization</a></b> - is the most common use case for JWTs, particularly popular with Single Sign-On (SSO).  After successful user login, each subsequent request includes the JWT for access.  This token allows the user to access permitted routes, services, and various resources.  The resource server evaluates the token's claims to grant appropriate permissions.</div></li><li><div><b><a href="https://www.evernote.com/shard/s341/nl/54341568/92ec4a78-199c-27e7-bb02-c8c126298d64?title=Authentication" rev="en_rl_none">Authentication</a> </b>- when a user successfully logs in, validating their provided credentials.  Upon successful verification, an ID token is returned to the client application.  According to OpenID Connect (OIDC) specifications, this ID token is always a JWT.  It securely asserts the user's identity for subsequent interactions.</div></li><li><div><b>Information Exchange</b> - a secure method for transmitting information reliably between various parties.  JWTs can be digitally signed using public-private key pairs for strong verification.  This cryptographic signing assures the recipient of the sender's asserted identity.  It effectively prevents impersonation and ensures message integrity across communication channels.</div></li></ul><div><b>Sub Types:</b></div><ul><li><div><b><a href="https://www.evernote.com/shard/s341/nl/54341568/b3cced87-e2d8-9ba1-e1de-f64cb3a34ae3?title=JSON%20Web%20Signature%20(JWS)" rev="en_rl_none">JSON Web Signature (JWS)</a> </b>- this type explicitly details content secured with a digital signature or MAC.  It comprises three distinct sections: the header, the payload, and the signature.  This specific structure ensures data integrity and authenticity for the token.</div></li><li><div><b>JSON Web Encryption (JWE) </b>- it consists of five critical parts for secure data transfer.  These components include the header, the encrypted key, and the initialization vector.  Additionally, it contains the ciphertext (payload) and an authentication tag.  This comprehensive structure ensures both confidentiality and integrity effectively.</div></li></ul><div><b><span style="--en-markholder:true;"><br/></span></b></div></en-note>]]></content>
  </note>
  <note>
    <title>JWT Signing Algorithm</title>
    <note-attributes>
    </note-attributes>
    <content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><div>The process of signing a JSON Web Token (JWT) ensures its integrity and verifiable authenticity.  This crucial step employs a cryptographic algorithm specified in the JWT header's <code>alg</code> field.  The primary signing algorithm types are symmetric and also asymmetric.  Selecting the right algorithm significantly impacts the overall security model for tokens.</div><div><br/></div><div><b>Types</b>:</div><ul><li><div><b><a href="https://www.evernote.com/shard/s341/nl/54341568/8e053a06-e366-a297-1ab9-33227f821572" rev="en_rl_none">Symmetric</a> - </b>a single, shared private key is used by both parties.  The authorization server employs this key to sign the token.  Conversely, the resource server uses the exact same key to verify the digital signature.  This shared secret simplifies key management but demands secure distribution.</div></li><li><div><b><a href="https://www.evernote.com/shard/s341/nl/54341568/181bf54b-c2d8-4dd1-9073-ea12b4932c5e" rev="en_rl_none">Asymmetric</a> </b>- a public and private cryptographic key pair is utilized for signing.  The authorization server signs the token using its highly secret private key.  Subsequently, the resource server uses the corresponding public key to verify the signature.  This public key can be safely shared without compromising system security.</div></li></ul><div><b>Algorithms:</b></div><ul><li><div><b><a href="https://www.evernote.com/shard/s341/nl/54341568/6c2210c2-d8be-c084-7e09-36688fd771ce?title=RSA%20SecurID" rev="en_rl_none">Rivest-Shamir-Adleman(RSA)</a> </b>- an asymmetric algorithm employing a public-private key pair for digital signatures.  It generates a unique signature with the private key, verifiable by the public key.  RSA is widely used for secure data transmission and authentication protocols.</div></li><li><div><b>Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm(ECDSA)</b> - an asymmetric algorithm utilizing a public and private key pair.  This method is specifically based on the mathematical principles of elliptic curve cryptography.  ECDSA offers strong security with significantly smaller key sizes, making it efficient.</div></li><li><div><b><a href="https://www.evernote.com/shard/s341/nl/54341568/583628e7-8a5a-40f2-9f64-0d1c0e1676ab?title=HMAC" rev="en_rl_none">Hash-based Message Authentication Code(HMAC)</a></b> - a symmetric algorithm that combines a cryptographic hash function and a secret key.  This combination is used to generate a unique digital signature.  The same secret key is vital for both signing and verification.</div></li></ul></en-note>]]></content>
  </note>
  <note>
    <title>JWT Components</title>
    <note-attributes>
    </note-attributes>
    <content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><div>The header section specifies the cryptographic algorithm for signature calculation.  The payload contains the actual data or claims exchanged between parties.  For OAuth, authorization servers include user authorities and client details.  A digital signature is computed using the encoded header and payload.  This signature leverages the hashing algorithm specified in the header.  In its compact form, JSON Web Tokens consist of three distinct dot-separated parts:</div><en-media hash="9445bca7ccc9a6bbbb091ce56da030f0" style="--en-naturalWidth:510; --en-naturalHeight:421;" type="image/png"/><div><b>Header</b> - typically consists of two primary parts for token definition.  The first specifies the token type, which is always JWT.  The second identifies the hashing algorithm being used, such as HMAC SHA256 or RSA.  For example, a common header structure appears as:</div><div>    {</div><div>"alg": "HS256",</div><div>"typ": "JWT"</div><div>    }</div><div><b>Payload</b> <b>(<a href="https://www.evernote.com/shard/s341/nl/54341568/b51a05e8-403a-1ead-f2fe-ef98e1738690?title=JWT%20Claims" rev="en_rl_none">JWT Claims</a>)</b> - contains the claims, which are essential statements about an entity.  Typically, these claims describe the user or provide additional metadata.  They are structured as key-value pairs, conveying critical information.</div><ul style="--en-todo:true;"><ul><li><div><b>registered</b> - these are a set of predefined claims, not mandatory but strongly recommended.  They standardize common token information for improved interoperability across systems.  Adhering to these registered claims improves system consistency significantly.</div></li><li><div><b>public</b> - these claims can be defined freely by implementers using JWTs.  However, they require proper registration to avoid naming collisions.  The IANA JSON Web Token Registry manages these public claims.</div></li><li><div><b>private</b> - these are custom claims designed for sharing information between specific, agreeing parties.  Their names are not centrally registered and can lead to collisions.  Therefore, careful coordination is essential for private claim usage.</div></li></ul></ul><div><b>Signature</b> - to create this part, combine the encoded header and payload.  Additionally, include a secret key and the algorithm specified in the header.  Then, sign this combined data using a chosen hashing algorithm.  For example, using the HMAC SHA256 algorithm involves:</div><div>                 HMACSHA256(</div><div>base64UrlEncode(header) + "." +</div><div>base64UrlEncode(payload), secret)</div></en-note>]]></content>
