  <note>
    <title>JWT Claims</title>
    <note-attributes>
    </note-attributes>
    <content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><div>The central information within a JSON Web Token (JWT) resides in its payload.  This payload contains claims, structured as essential name-value pairs.  Claims are fundamental for both authentication and authorization decisions.  During a request, a server examines these claims carefully.  This verification confirms the user's identity and determines granted permissions.  Consequently, the server decides what actions or data the user can access.</div><div><br/></div><div><b>Types</b>:</div><ul><li style="--en-firstLetterMarksLength:1;"><div><b>Registered</b> (Most Important) - these are a set of predefined, standardized claims.  They are not mandatory for use but are highly recommended.  While their usage is optional, their specific definitions must be obeyed strictly.  There are currently only seven such officially registered claims within the standard.</div></li><ul><li style="--en-firstLetterMarksLength:1;"><div><b>aud </b>- a string or array of strings identifying the intended recipients.  If the token's recipient is not listed, the token must be rejected.  This ensures the token is only processed by its target audience.</div></li><li style="--en-firstLetterMarksLength:1;"><div><b>iss </b>- identity provider, which is the entity that issued the JWT.  This is typically an authentication server or a similar service.  It explicitly identifies the trusted issuer of the token within the system.</div></li><li style="--en-firstLetterMarksLength:1;"><div><b>iat </b>- is the timestamp representing the time when the JWT was issued.  It is always expressed as seconds since the Unix Epoch.  This claim helps effectively determine the token's creation age for validation.</div></li><li style="--en-firstLetterMarksLength:1;"><div><b>sub </b>- subject of the JWT, which typically represents the user.  This claim uniquely identifies the principal that is the subject of the token.  It specifies the entity about which the token asserts information.</div></li><li style="--en-firstLetterMarksLength:1;"><div><b>exp </b>- contains the token's expiration timestamp, after which it becomes invalid.  If a received token has expired, an unauthorized response must be returned.  This ensures tokens are only valid for their intended lifespan.</div></li><li style="--en-firstLetterMarksLength:1;"><div><b>nbf </b>- not before, indicating the earliest time the token is valid for processing.  The token must not be issued or accepted prior to this date.  It prevents usage before a specific point in time.</div></li><li style="--en-firstLetterMarksLength:1;"><div><b>jti </b>- the unique token identifier, providing a specific ID for the JWT.  This claim can prevent replay attacks by ensuring tokens are used only once.  It allows for efficient token tracking and revocation management.</div></li></ul><li style="--en-firstLetterMarksLength:1;"><div><b>Custom</b> - consists of both non-registered public and private claims.  Public claims aim to be collision-resistant, requiring official registration with IANA.  Conversely, private claims are prone to naming collisions without strict coordination.  Careful management of custom claims is crucial for system stability.</div></li></ul><div><b>Access</b>: for both registered and custom claims within a JWT.  This refers to how these claims are structured and categorized.  Understanding this claim access helps in proper token processing effectively.</div><ul><li style="--en-firstLetterMarksLength:1;"><div><b>Public </b>- these are claims officially available and listed on the <a href="https://www.iana.org/assignments/jwt/jwt.xhtml" rev="en_rl_none"><span style="color:rgb(24, 168, 65);">IANA JSON Web Token Registry</span></a>.  They are intended for interoperability across different implementations.  Significantly, the first seven entries in this registry are the registered claims.</div></li><li style="--en-firstLetterMarksLength:1;"><div><b>Private </b>- these are custom claims designed for sharing information between specific, agreeing parties.  These claims might not be universally recognized by different applications.  Therefore, public or registered claims should be preferred for broader compatibility.  Careful consideration is needed for their implementation and scope.</div></li></ul></en-note>]]></content>
  </note>
  <note>
    <title>Using OAuth2 with JWT</title>
    <note-attributes>
    </note-attributes>
    <content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><div><br/></div><div><b>Setup:</b></div><ul><li><div><b><a href="https://share.evernote.com/note/07fa799c-2ed5-53b6-4072-ee45318ec205" rev="en_rl_none">Resource Server</a></b> - API that provides access to a requested protected resource.  It achieves this by verifying the user's information presented in a JWT.  This server enforces authorization rules based on token claims.</div></li><ol><li><div>add Spring Security and OAuth2 Resource Server dependencies to pom.xml.  These dependencies enable JWT processing capabilities for the resource server.  Proper configuration ensures secure resource protection and validation effectively.</div></li><li><div>configure Spring Security to validate all incoming JWT tokens.  This crucial step involves setting up appropriate filters and decoders.  It ensures only legitimate tokens securely grant access to resources.</div></li><li><div>use a JwtDecoder to validate incoming JWT tokens.  This decoder specifically processes tokens issued by the authorization server.  It rigorously verifies the token's signature and its contained claims.  Correct validation is paramount for maintaining robust system security.</div></li><li><div>configure token validation properties to align with strict security requirements.  This involves setting expiration times and trusted issuer details.  Proper configuration is vital for robust token security implementations.</div></li></ol><li><div><b><a href="https://share.evernote.com/note/f631befa-56d1-58a7-459c-db33edba26aa" rev="en_rl_none">Authorization Server</a></b> - what the user interacts with when an application requests account access.  It manages the user's consent and securely issues access tokens.  This server is crucial for secure and compliant authorization flows.</div></li><ol><li><div>add Spring Security and OAuth2 Authorization Server to pom.xml.  These dependencies are essential for building a robust authorization server.  They provide the necessary framework components for token management.  This setup enables secure token issuance and revocation mechanisms.</div></li><li><div>configure an authorization server by extending AuthorizationServerConfigurerAdapter.  Alternatively, utilize the Spring Security DSL for concise configuration.  This defines how clients interact and tokens are securely issued.  Proper setup is essential for overall system security and compliance.</div></li><li><div>set up the JWT encoder for the Authorization Server.  This component enables the server to digitally sign issued tokens.  The encoder ensures token integrity and authenticity effectively.  It's a critical part of the token issuance process.</div></li></ol></ul><div><br/></div><div><b>Authorization Server Project</b>:</div><ol><li><div>Authorization Config</div></li><ol><li><div>extend the JWTAccessTokenConverter to create JWT tokens.  This is done instead of producing regular OAuth tokens.  It ensures the token format strictly adheres to JWT specifications.  This customization is vital for robust JWT-based authentication systems.</div></li><li><div>store the tokens using a <a href="https://www.evernote.com/shard/s341/nl/54341568/834e975c-395f-dca2-e5bb-f00c6204b127" rel="noopener noreferrer" rev="en_rl_none">JwtTokenStore</a> implementation.  Despite its name, it inherently operates in a stateless manner.  It primarily converts JWTs into authentication objects for system processing.  This strategy effectively avoids server-side token persistence for enhanced scalability.</div></li></ol></ol><div><br/></div><div><b><a href="https://www.baeldung.com/spring-security-oauth-jwt">Ex) Authorization Server and Resource Server</a></b> -</div></en-note>]]></content>
  </note>
  <note>
    <title>JwtTokenStore</title>
    <note-attributes>
    </note-attributes>
    <content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><div>A JwtTokenStore is a component within Spring Security OAuth 2.0.  It is specifically designed for working with JSON Web Tokens (JWTs) effectively.  This component operates in a stateless manner, avoiding token data persistence.  Consequently, retrieval methods will always return null, indicating no stored state.  Its primary role is to convert between access tokens and authentication objects.  This enables systems requiring a TokenStore to function without persistent storage.  For proper operation, the same JwtAccessTokenConverter instance must be used.  This converter both creates the original JWT and also verifies it.  This ensures consistency and maintains trust in all processed tokens.</div><div><br/></div><div><b>Characteristics</b>:</div><ul><li><div><b>Stateless </b>- JwtTokenStore does not persist any token information itself.  All necessary token details, including user identity and scopes, are encoded directly within the JWT.  This design principle enhances scalability and reduces server load.</div></li><li><div><b>Verification </b>- it relies on a JwtAccessTokenConverter for decoding and signature verification.  This crucial component ensures the authenticity and integrity of the JWT.  Proper verification guarantees the token has not been tampered with.</div></li><li><div><b>No Persistent Storage </b>- because token information is self-contained and cryptographically signed.  The server does not need to maintain a separate store of active tokens.  This design significantly simplifies system architecture and improves overall scalability.  It eliminates the overhead of stateful token management efficiently.</div></li></ul><div><b>Functionality</b>:</div><ul><li><div><b>Token Extraction</b> - when a protected resource is accessed using a JWT.  The JwtTokenStore utilizes the JwtAccessTokenConverter for this extraction process.  It extracts authentication details, typically an OAuth2Authentication object, from the token.  This provides the necessary context for subsequent authorization decisions.</div></li><li><div><b>Token Revocation</b>: is more challenging with a JwtTokenStore than a stateful store.  Since the server lacks an active token list, direct invalidation is difficult.  An issued JWT cannot be revoked before its natural expiration time.  Common strategies for handling JWT revocation with practical mechanisms include:</div></li><ul><li><div><b>Blacklisting </b>- implementing a separate blacklist to track individually revoked tokens.  This strategy effectively adds a stateful element to an otherwise stateless system.  It requires maintaining a persistent store of invalid tokens.</div></li><li><div><b>Updating Signing Keys</b> - periodically changing the cryptographic signing key.  This action effectively invalidates all previously issued tokens.  It forces clients to reauthenticate for new valid tokens.  This is particularly useful for mass revocation scenarios.</div></li><li><div><b>Short Expiration Times</b> - issuing tokens with shorter, limited lifespans.  This significantly reduces the window for a compromised token's misuse.  It minimizes the impact of potential security breaches effectively.  Refresh tokens can manage user experience without constant reauthentication seamlessly.</div></li></ul></ul></en-note>]]></content>
  </note>
  <note>
    <title>JSON Web Key (JWK)</title>
    <note-attributes>
    </note-attributes>
    <content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><div>Both JSON Web Tokens (JWT) and JSON Web Keys (JWK) are integral for secure web communication.  A JWT is a compact, URL-safe string that transmits information securely.  It consists of a header, a payload, and a critical signature.  The signature, a core security component, is generated with a cryptographic algorithm and a key.  Here, the JWK becomes essential for understanding the process.  A JWK represents the key itself in a standardized JSON format.  It describes a cryptographic key, detailing its type (e.g., RSA, Elliptic Curve).  Furthermore, it specifies its intended use (e.g., signing or encryption) and unique ID.  Within a JWT context, a JWK is the cryptographic key used for signature creation.  Services verify JWT authenticity by using the public key from the corresponding JWK.  This relationship ensures token integrity verification by recipients with correct key access.  Keys are often shared in a standardized JSON Web Key Set (JWKS) format.</div><div><br/></div><div><b>Components</b>:</div><ul><li><div><code><b>kty</b></code><b> (Key Type) </b>- a mandatory parameter identifying the cryptographic algorithm family.  This includes types like RSA for RSA keys or EC for Elliptic Curve keys.  It explicitly specifies the fundamental type of key being represented.  This is crucial for proper key interpretation and usage.</div></li><li><div><code><b>alg</b></code><b> (Algorithm)</b> - this parameter specifies the particular cryptographic algorithm for key use.  Examples include RS256 for RSA signatures or ES256 for ECDSA.  It clearly defines the intended cryptographic operation for the key.  This ensures compatibility and correct processing across different systems.</div></li><li><div><code><b>use</b></code><b> (Public Key Use)</b> - indicates the intended purpose or usage of the cryptographic key.  This might be sig for a digital signature or enc for encryption.  It helps recipients understand the key's specific function.  This parameter is crucial for proper key application in security operations.</div></li><li><div><code><b>key_ops</b></code><b> (Key Operations) </b>- an optional parameter providing a granular list of permitted key operations.  For example, it might specify <code>["sign", "verify"]</code> for signature purposes.  This offers more precise control than the use parameter.  It enhances security by explicitly limiting key functionality.</div></li><li><div><code><b>kid</b></code><b> (Key ID)</b> - an optional but highly recommended unique identifier for the key.  It is particularly useful for managing multiple keys within a JSON Web Key Set (JWKS).  This ID allows efficient key lookup and seamless rotation.  It significantly simplifies key management in complex distributed systems.</div></li></ul><div><br/></div><div>Ex)</div><div style='--en-codeblock:true; --en-syntaxLanguage:json; --en-lineWrapping:false;box-sizing: border-box; padding: 8px; font-family: "Fira Code","Consolas","Monaco","Andale Mono","Ubuntu Mono","Courier New",monospace font-size: 12px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); border-top-left-radius: 4px; border-top-right-radius: 4px; border-bottom-right-radius: 4px; border-bottom-left-radius: 4px; background-color: rgb(251, 250, 248); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.14902); background-position: initial initial; background-repeat: initial initial;'><div>{</div><div>    "alg":"RSA",</div><div>    "mod":"0vx7agoebGcQSuuPiLJXZptN9nndrQmbXEps2aiAFbWhM78LhWx4cbbfAAtVT86zwu1RK7aPFFxuhDR1</div><div>        L6tSoc_BJECPebWKRXjBZCiFV4n3oknjhMstn64tZ_2W-5JsGY4Hc5n9yBXArwl93lqt7_RN5w6Cf0h4QyQ</div><div>        5v-65YGjQR0_FDW2QvzqY368QQMicAtaSqzs8KJZgnYb9c7d0zgdAZHzu6qMQvRL5hajrn1n91CbOpbI</div><div>        SD08qNLyrdkt-bFTWhAI4vMQFh6WeZu0fM4lFd2NcRwr3XPksINHaQ-G_xBniIqbw0Ls1jF44-csFCur-              kEgU8awapJzKnqDKgw",</div><div>    "exp":"AQAB",</div><div>    "kid":"2011-04-29"</div><div>}</div></div><div><br/></div></en-note>]]></content>
  </note>
  <note>
    <title>JWT Authentication Flow</title>
    <note-attributes>
    </note-attributes>
    <content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><div><br/></div><div><b>SSO Flow:</b></div><ol><li><div><b>Authentication</b> - user provides their login credentials, such as username and password, to the server.  The server then securely verifies these credentials for validity.  Successful authentication reliably establishes the user's identity for subsequent actions.</div></li><li><div><b>Token Generation</b> - upon successful authentication, the server generates a JSON Web Token (JWT).  This token contains essential information about the user, known as claims.  The server digitally signs the JWT to ensure its integrity and authenticity.  This prevents any unauthorized tampering with the token.</div></li><li><div><b>Token Issuance</b> - server sends the newly generated JWT back to the client application.  This token is then used for future authenticated requests.  It establishes a secure session context for the user efficiently.</div></li><li><div><b>Token Storage</b> - client stores the received JWT securely.  Common storage locations include local storage or HTTP-only cookies.  Secure storage prevents unauthorized access to the token effectively.  This is vital for client-side security and protecting user sessions.</div></li><li><div><b>Resource Access </b>- when the client requests access to a protected resource.  It includes the JWT in the HTTP Authorization header.  This is typically done using the Bearer schema securely.  The server then uses this token for authorization decisions and access control.</div></li><li><div><b>Token Verification</b> - the server receives the client's request containing the JWT.  It then verifies the JWT's digital signature for authenticity.  This uses its secret key or the corresponding public key.  Such verification ensures the token has not been tampered with.</div></li><li><div><b>Authorization </b>- if the signature is valid, the server decodes the JWT.  It then retrieves user information and associated claims.  Based on these claims, the server accurately determines user authorization.  This dictates access to the requested protected resource effectively.  Effective authorization is crucial for maintaining robust system security.</div></li><li><div><b>Response </b>- server processes the request after successful authorization.  It then returns the appropriate response data to the client.  This completes the entire single sign-on flow interaction successfully.</div></li></ol></en-note>]]></content>
  </note>
  <note>
    <title>JSON Web Signature (JWS)</title>
    <note-attributes>
    </note-attributes>
    <content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><div>A JSON Web Signature (JWS) is a compact, URL-safe data structure.  It represents content secured with a digital signature or a Message Authentication Code (MAC).  JWS is a key component of the JSON Web Token (JWT) standard.  Its core purpose is to ensure data integrity and authenticity.  A JWS comprises three parts: a header, a payload, and a signature.  All parts are Base64url-encoded and concatenated with periods.  The header specifies the cryptographic algorithm used for signing operations.  The payload, conversely, contains the actual data or claims.  The signature is generated by applying the algorithm to the encoded header and payload.  This process involves a shared secret or private key.  It allows recipients to verify data has not been tampered with.  Furthermore, it confirms the data originates from a trusted source.  The JWS standard is formally defined in RFC 7515.</div></en-note>]]></content>
  </note>
  <note>
    <title>JWT Security</title>
    <note-attributes>
    </note-attributes>
    <content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><div>The security of a JSON Web Token (JWT) relies heavily on its digital signature.  This signature ensures that the information contained within has not been altered.  A strong focus on signed tokens, known as JSON Web Signatures (JWS), is paramount.  This guarantees the integrity and authenticity of all carried claims.  While a JWT can be encrypted to conceal contents from unauthorized parties, this is not universally true.  For instance, JWTs issued by Auth0 are signed but not encrypted.  Their contents are visible, yet their authenticity remains fully verifiable.  Encryption, however, offers an additional layer of confidentiality.</div><div><br/></div><div><b>Key Security Points</b>:</div><ul><li><div>use RS256 or other stronger cryptographic algorithms for signing.  These provide robust security for digital signatures effectively.  Selecting appropriate algorithms is crucial for comprehensive token protection.</div></li><li><div>securely manage and regularly rotate all cryptographic keys.  Avoid hardcoding secrets directly within application code.  This practice minimizes exposure risks significantly and enhances security.  Implement automated key rotation processes for best practice.</div></li><li><div>limit token lifespan to a short, reasonable duration.  Utilize refresh tokens for extended session management securely.  This minimizes the impact of a potentially compromised token effectively.  It significantly enhances overall system security posture.</div></li><li><div>avoid storing sensitive data directly within the JWT payload.  Encrypt the payload if sensitive information is absolutely necessary.  This adds a crucial layer of confidentiality protection.  Prioritize minimal data exposure always for enhanced security.</div></li><li><div>transmit tokens exclusively over HTTPS to prevent eavesdropping.  Store them in HTTP-only cookies or other secure client-side locations.  This mitigates risks like Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks.  Always prioritize encrypted and secure transmission mechanisms diligently.</div></li><li><div>implement robust server-side validation for all incoming tokens.  This includes checking token expiration and validating all claims.  Such validation is critical for comprehensive security assurance.  It effectively prevents the use of invalid or tampered tokens.</div></li><li><div>log all token-related events comprehensively for auditing purposes.  Monitor activity for any unusual or suspicious patterns closely.  This proactive approach helps detect potential compromises efficiently.  Timely detection is vital for effective incident response strategies.</div></li></ul><div><br/></div></en-note>]]></content>
  </note>
  <note>
    <title>JWT Authorization Flow</title>
    <note-attributes>
    </note-attributes>
    <content><![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><div>Upon successful login, a server issues a JSON Web Token (JWT) to the user.  The user includes this token in the Authorization header for subsequent requests.  This is essential for accessing protected resources securely.  It is typically done using the Bearer schema.  The Bearer schema signifies the token grants authority to its holder.  This mechanism ensures only authorized users access protected resources correctly.</div><en-media hash="bbd80b6b343ea31ab895e7f9502ed4f9" style="--en-naturalWidth:489; --en-naturalHeight:176;" type="image/png"/><div><b>Flow</b>:</div><ol><li><div>The client application requests authorization from the <a href="https://www.evernote.com/shard/s341/nl/54341568/f631befa-56d1-58a7-459c-db33edba26aa?title=Authorization%20Server" rev="en_rl_none">authorization server</a>.  This process is executed through one of several authorization flows.  For example, an <a href="http://openid.net/connect/" rev="en_rl_none" shape="rect" target="_blank">OpenID Connect</a> compliant web application typically uses the <code><span style="font-size: 14px;">/oauth/authorize</span></code> endpoint.  It specifically leverages the <a href="http://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-core-1_0.html#CodeFlowAuth" rev="en_rl_none" shape="rect" target="_blank">authorization code flow</a> for secure grants.  This multi-step exchange effectively ensures delegated access to resources.</div></li><li><div>When authorization is granted, the authorization server returns an access token.  This token is then provided directly to the client application.  It empowers the client to access protected resources.  This is a critical step in the OAuth flow.</div></li><li><div>The application then uses this access token for authentication.  It presents the token to access a protected resource.  This resource might be an API or another external service.  Proper token usage ensures secure communication between parties effectively and reliably.</div></li></ol><div><br/></div><div><br/></div><div><br/></div></en-note>]]></content>
    <resource>
      <data encoding="base64">
iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAekAAACwCAYAAAArOJYcAAAAAXNSR0IArs4c6QAAAARnQU1BAACxjwv8YQUAAAAJcEhZcwAADsQAAA7EAZUrDhsAAEFJSURBVHhe7Z0FfFRX9sd/o5m4EZJAgltwd6feYoVS2Zb+d0tt29Lt7lLduuzWqCsuRYu7u7trgCR43DMT+59zZyZMQgiWhJnkfPk88t59790nc9/53XNVk09AEARBEASnQ2v7KwiCIAiC
